    "result_text": "The full build passes. Done.\n\n## What I did\n\nAddress normalization now runs whenever an owner is created, following the exact pattern the codebase already uses for telephone normalization (`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`).\n\n**Two files touched:**\n\n1. **New `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java`** \u2014 a small, single-purpose function class (no surrounding class complexity to hold). It takes the built `Owner` via `@Val` and mutates its address in place: trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, and expands `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `normalizeAddress` step into the create pipeline, right after `normalizeTelephone` and before the uniqueness checks and save.\n\n**Why nothing else needed changing** (keeping structural impact minimal):\n\n- Because the step mutates the stored `Owner.address` in place *before* `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold`, `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`, and `SaveOwner` run, every downstream comparison \u2014 household duplicate detection and the shared household id \u2014 automatically operates on the normalized address. Existing owners were normalized at their own creation time, so comparisons stay consistent. I did not have to edit `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` at all (zero cost on that larger class).\n- The blank-after-normalization rejection is already enforced: the `OwnerFields.address` schema carries `minLength: 1` and pattern `.*\\S.*`, so a whitespace-only address fails `@Valid` in `BuildOwner` and returns 400 before normalization. Since collapsing/upper-casing/expanding can never turn a non-blank value blank, the required-field check is equivalent to \"blank after normalization\" \u2014 no extra exception/handler/YAML files needed.\n\nAll tests green: the 3 new acceptance tests (`coreNormalizesWhitespaceAndCase`, `functionalityExpandsAbbreviations`, `errorRejectsBlankAfterNormalize`) and the full suite build successfully.",
